Here is the history of Ginsu the Samurai, the luckiest nethacker in history:

Possible Spoilers ahead (nothing major)


Poor Ginsu started out a feeble first level samurai, with the whole dungeon
ahead of him.  I began exploring, and in the first room after the stairs was
an altar.  I thought "cool", and immediately kicked my pet to death (usually
good for terrible luck), and got no corpse to sacrifice :(

Continuing on, I found a wand in the next room.  Being wise to the ways of 
nethack, I engraved on the floor and was greeted with this message:

You may wish for an object:

After my heart started up again, I wished for 3 blessed scrolls of charging,
but got only one.  Paranoid soul that I am, I wandered back to the altar
and dropped it...it was cursed!

I elected to save my remaining wishes and look for some water to bless at the
altar, so away down the stairs I went.  In the gnome mines, a poisoned orc
arrow hit me at the frail level of 5, and I was reduced to one hit point!
Paniced by the nearness of death, I prayed for salvation, and was restored
to full health.  Murmuring thanks to my merciful god, I fled up the stairs
to make my holy water.

At the first fountain I found, I begin dipping potions.  In went the brown
potion, out came a water demon!  I thought damn, there goes the game, but
wait....

The water demon is grateful for her release!  She grants you a wish!

Marvel upon marvels!  I wished for speed boots, that I might flee the next
disaster without risk to myself.  I then dipped again, and unleashed a second
demon!  This one was not as friendly, so I took advantage of my newfound 
footwear to head up to level 2.  Dipping my next potion at a fountain on that
level, a THIRD water demon came out.  It seemed my game was doomed, but....

The water demon is grateful for his release!  He grants you a wish!

This time I was a bit more pragmatic, and wished for a shiny new (stainless
steel with lifetime sharpness) Excalibur, in case my next dip also unleashed
a demon.  Fortunately, it didn't, and I went back to my altar with 4 potions
of water ready for blessing.

On level 1, a wererat attacked me, and summoned his vermin-infested pack to
aid him.  Excalibur quickly dispatched of this offal, and I took the four
resulting rat corpses to the altar, that my god might be pleased with my
efforts.

The first offering converted the altar, and the second produced an object...
none other than the famed sword Snickersnee!  I shrugged, and shoved it into
my backpack in case I lost Excalibur.  The next two corpses (and a wandering
goblin) restored my god's favor, and I blessed the potions, and sat down
for some serious wishing.

After leaving the altar, I was not yet level 7, and I already had used 
8 wishes, including GDSM, gauntlets of power, and a shield of reflection.
(and of course an amulet of life saving)

I reversed genocided tengus three times, and got telportitis but no t-control.
:(  But guess what the first ring I found was!

On the way down I acquired the Samurai's blade (those who have had it know
how good it is) and another wish from a throne, which all told added up to
14 wishes when I got the wand in the castle. Nothing really threatened me
until I got down to the lowest reaches of hell and the endgame.

Amusing side note:  In the Valley of the Dead, I fought a vampire.  I had 
already genocided vampire lords for the hell of it, and this vampire
drank a potion of gain level.

The vampire drinks a potion of gain level!
The vampire turns into a vampire lord.
The vampire dies suddenly!

:) One of the more amusing ways I've seen to die.

Due to a lack of levitation I had to us boots of jumping to cross the medusa
level, and then blew out the castle door with a wand of striking and jumped
in :)  Oh the humiliation of it all!


So, anyone else ever have a game start out that well, espec. one that began
with killing my pet? :)

After killing the Wizard and getting the amulet of Yendor, I made my way out of
the dungeon (Asmodeus and Jubilex were also causualties), and played through
the endgame.  Talked about tough!

Ginsu the Shogun ascended with damn near every item in the game (still missing
that blessed tinning kit....), all the intrinsics save protection, and the
Hand of Elbereth. He also knew 30 different spells, and had at least 50 gems
(pretty bad, I know)

You and the invisible Wizard of Yendor went to you reward 4593412 points.
58786 gold, 61069 moves
You were level 30 with a maximum of 295 hit points when you ascended.


Time to try another class, thank god I finally ascended :)

 

